WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.033 --> 00:00:00.734
Question Is it

00:00:00.734 --> 00:00:02.335
appropriate for law enforcement

00:00:02.335 --> 00:00:03.737
to monitor social media

00:00:03.737 --> 00:00:05.872
to prevent and solve crimes?

00:00:05.872 --> 00:00:07.407
I ask because here in Philadelphia

00:00:07.407 --> 00:00:08.675
this week, parts of the city

00:00:08.675 --> 00:00:09.743
were ransacked

00:00:09.743 --> 00:00:11.745
by widespread smash and grab looting.

00:00:12.078 --> 00:00:13.847
The thieves targeted clothing and sneaker

00:00:13.847 --> 00:00:14.514
shops, high

00:00:14.514 --> 00:00:16.016
end stores, wine and spirits

00:00:16.016 --> 00:00:18.752
shops, pharmacies and an Apple Store.

00:00:18.785 --> 00:00:21.121
Police say the agitators organize

00:00:21.121 --> 00:00:23.356
their plans on social media.

00:00:23.356 --> 00:00:24.924
Among the more than 50 folks

00:00:24.924 --> 00:00:26.993
arrested was an influencer.

00:00:27.027 --> 00:00:29.162
Deja Blackwell, known as Meatball

00:00:29.462 --> 00:00:30.430
According to police,

00:00:30.430 --> 00:00:32.432
she livestreamed the ransacking

00:00:32.432 --> 00:00:33.500
and broadcast,

00:00:33.500 --> 00:00:35.802
which stores and locations to loot

00:00:37.771 --> 00:00:39.305
That's not funny.

00:00:39.572 --> 00:00:42.308
The cops come and they go

00:00:54.287 --> 00:00:56.556
which means we're going

00:00:56.556 --> 00:00:58.892
for the second suite

00:01:00.560 --> 00:01:01.027
We're

00:01:06.466 --> 00:01:07.400
everybody's lucky.

00:01:07.400 --> 00:01:09.436
Everybody must be outraged by the

00:01:12.038 --> 00:01:13.306
she was later arrested

00:01:13.306 --> 00:01:13.740
after what

00:01:13.740 --> 00:01:14.174
the Philadelphia

00:01:14.174 --> 00:01:15.842
Inquirer dubbed a digital cat

00:01:15.842 --> 00:01:17.243
and mouse game with police.

00:01:17.243 --> 00:01:18.511
She now faces eight charges,

00:01:18.511 --> 00:01:20.847
including criminal conspiracy and riot.

00:01:20.847 --> 00:01:22.816
She declined CNN's request for comment,

00:01:22.816 --> 00:01:24.818
but later told a local TV station

00:01:24.818 --> 00:01:26.352
that her lawyer had advised her

00:01:26.352 --> 00:01:27.587
not to do interviews.

00:01:27.587 --> 00:01:28.888
What happened in Philadelphia

00:01:28.888 --> 00:01:30.290
isn't alone. Incident.

00:01:30.290 --> 00:01:31.491
Not a one off.

00:01:31.491 --> 00:01:32.125
Cities across

00:01:32.125 --> 00:01:32.492
the country

00:01:32.492 --> 00:01:33.626
are working to combat

00:01:33.626 --> 00:01:35.195
organized retail crime

00:01:35.195 --> 00:01:36.796
and performance crime,

00:01:36.796 --> 00:01:37.697
where perpetrators

00:01:37.697 --> 00:01:38.998
often brag about their actions

00:01:38.998 --> 00:01:40.300
on social media,

00:01:40.300 --> 00:01:41.734
with more people posting crimes

00:01:41.734 --> 00:01:44.204
publicly online or using social media

00:01:44.204 --> 00:01:45.772
as a criminal tool.

00:01:45.772 --> 00:01:47.107
Police departments are stressing

00:01:47.107 --> 00:01:47.640
the importance

00:01:47.640 --> 00:01:49.075
of ramping up surveillance

00:01:49.075 --> 00:01:50.910
so that they can monitor accounts

00:01:50.910 --> 00:01:52.679
for threats or criminal activity.

00:01:52.679 --> 00:01:54.814
But they're facing stiff opposition

00:01:54.814 --> 00:01:56.282
from civil liberty advocates

00:01:56.282 --> 00:01:57.050
who say that it's

00:01:57.050 --> 00:01:58.318
just another way for the government

00:01:58.318 --> 00:02:00.386
to infringe on your right to privacy.

00:02:00.887 --> 00:02:02.922
Take Boston as just one example.

00:02:02.922 --> 00:02:03.890
In 2016,

00:02:03.890 --> 00:02:06.392
the city wanted to spend $1.4 million

00:02:06.693 --> 00:02:09.162
to beef up its social media monitoring

00:02:09.696 --> 00:02:10.864
Butt kicked it down the road

00:02:10.864 --> 00:02:12.999
after backlash from the ACLU,

00:02:12.999 --> 00:02:13.466
who said that

00:02:13.466 --> 00:02:14.734
it would chill free speech

00:02:14.734 --> 00:02:16.102
and could target communities

00:02:16.102 --> 00:02:18.271
already subjected to extra scrutiny,

00:02:18.271 --> 00:02:20.640
including Muslims and people of color.

00:02:20.640 --> 00:02:22.675
New York has ramped up their monitoring,

00:02:22.675 --> 00:02:23.943
even signing a contract

00:02:23.943 --> 00:02:25.145
with a company that uses

00:02:25.145 --> 00:02:27.547
AI to monitor online behavior.

00:02:27.780 --> 00:02:28.748
And in 2021,

00:02:28.748 --> 00:02:29.883
a public records request

00:02:29.883 --> 00:02:30.783
by the Brennan Center

00:02:30.783 --> 00:02:32.919
found the Los Angeles Police Department

00:02:32.919 --> 00:02:35.088
authorizes its officers to engage

00:02:35.088 --> 00:02:37.457
in extensive surveillance of social media

00:02:37.457 --> 00:02:39.325
without internal monitoring.

00:02:39.325 --> 00:02:39.926
Of the nature

00:02:39.926 --> 00:02:42.028
or effectiveness of the searches.

00:02:42.028 --> 00:02:43.897
The LAPD responded by saying

00:02:43.897 --> 00:02:45.098
that it works to keep residents

00:02:45.098 --> 00:02:46.366
safe and is committed

00:02:46.366 --> 00:02:48.034
to protecting their privacy rights

00:02:48.034 --> 00:02:50.103
as we confront the challenge every day.

00:02:50.603 --> 00:02:51.137
With me now

00:02:51.137 --> 00:02:53.139
to discuss is Pfizer Patel,

00:02:53.373 --> 00:02:55.141
the senior director of the Liberty

00:02:55.141 --> 00:02:56.876
and National Security Program

00:02:56.876 --> 00:02:58.778
at the Brennan Center for Justice

00:02:58.778 --> 00:03:00.380
Thank you so much for being here.

00:03:00.380 --> 00:03:01.014
To me,

00:03:01.014 --> 00:03:03.683
it seems natural, intuitive, logical

00:03:03.883 --> 00:03:04.884
that law enforcement

00:03:04.884 --> 00:03:05.752
would be following

00:03:05.752 --> 00:03:07.220
the social media accounts

00:03:07.220 --> 00:03:08.121
of individuals

00:03:08.121 --> 00:03:10.156
they know are suspect or involved

00:03:10.156 --> 00:03:11.491
in this kind of activity.

00:03:11.491 --> 00:03:12.592
And if they say, you know,

00:03:12.592 --> 00:03:13.760
we're headed to the Apple Store

00:03:13.760 --> 00:03:14.861
on Walnut Street,

00:03:14.861 --> 00:03:16.563
police ought to be there to meet them.

00:03:16.563 --> 00:03:17.630
What am I getting wrong?

00:03:18.932 --> 00:03:21.000
You're not getting anything wrong.

00:03:21.000 --> 00:03:23.069
It's just that that's not what.

00:03:23.570 --> 00:03:23.770
Well,

00:03:23.770 --> 00:03:25.805
that's not the only thing that police use

00:03:25.805 --> 00:03:28.208
social media for. And that's the problem.

00:03:28.541 --> 00:03:30.643
So if you think about investigating

00:03:30.643 --> 00:03:31.978
a crime, for example.

00:03:31.978 --> 00:03:32.478
Right,

00:03:32.478 --> 00:03:35.782
you know, of some kind of a criminal ring

00:03:36.015 --> 00:03:38.785
that is posting things on social media

00:03:38.785 --> 00:03:40.320
that might be relevant

00:03:40.320 --> 00:03:41.854
to your investigation.

00:03:41.854 --> 00:03:42.388
Of course,

00:03:42.388 --> 00:03:44.324
the cops should be able to look at that.

00:03:44.324 --> 00:03:45.258
If you know

00:03:45.258 --> 00:03:47.493
that there is a protest

00:03:47.493 --> 00:03:49.596
or a demonstration organized somewhere

00:03:49.896 --> 00:03:51.130
and the cops want to know, well,

00:03:51.130 --> 00:03:51.931
how many people do

00:03:51.931 --> 00:03:53.700
we need to have out there?

00:03:53.700 --> 00:03:55.001
What is the potential

00:03:55.001 --> 00:03:56.769
that things might get out of hand?

00:03:56.769 --> 00:03:59.005
What is our resource allocation?

00:03:59.005 --> 00:04:00.273
Absolutely.

00:04:00.273 --> 00:04:01.374
You need to be able to look

00:04:01.374 --> 00:04:03.142
at social media to do that.

00:04:03.142 --> 00:04:04.544
Unfortunately,

00:04:04.544 --> 00:04:06.713
because the rules are so lax

00:04:06.913 --> 00:04:07.313
Police

00:04:07.313 --> 00:04:09.682
also use social media to monitor

00:04:09.682 --> 00:04:11.217
protest movements.

00:04:11.217 --> 00:04:12.585
You mentioned the Boston

00:04:12.585 --> 00:04:15.288
Police Department's attempt to buy,

00:04:15.288 --> 00:04:15.788
you know,

00:04:15.788 --> 00:04:17.790
a $1.4 million contract

00:04:17.790 --> 00:04:19.325
for social media monitoring.

00:04:19.325 --> 00:04:20.827
Why did it get.

00:04:20.827 --> 00:04:22.061
Why did it get derailed?

00:04:22.061 --> 00:04:22.662
In part

00:04:22.662 --> 00:04:25.431
because evidence came out that the BPD

00:04:25.832 --> 00:04:27.667
was actually monitoring

00:04:27.667 --> 00:04:29.736
Black Lives Matter hashtags

00:04:29.736 --> 00:04:31.371
as well as what they called

00:04:31.371 --> 00:04:32.372
hashtag Muslim

00:04:32.372 --> 00:04:33.306
Lives Matter

00:04:33.306 --> 00:04:34.440
and other political,

00:04:34.440 --> 00:04:36.609
religious and racial protests.

00:04:36.609 --> 00:04:37.710
Categories.

00:04:37.710 --> 00:04:39.846
The same thing is true with the LAPD.

00:04:40.113 --> 00:04:41.781
It's not that we were worried

00:04:41.781 --> 00:04:42.915
that they're going to be using it

00:04:42.915 --> 00:04:44.117
to solve crimes.

00:04:44.117 --> 00:04:46.452
We were worried because we found evidence

00:04:46.653 --> 00:04:48.121
that they were using it

00:04:48.121 --> 00:04:50.423
to monitor protest activities.

00:04:50.456 --> 00:04:51.758
So what if there's overlap

00:04:51.758 --> 00:04:54.093
between the two, between what's at

00:04:55.128 --> 00:04:55.528
Pfizer?

00:04:55.528 --> 00:04:56.362
What if there's

00:04:56.362 --> 00:04:58.998
overlap between the two in the examples

00:04:58.998 --> 00:05:00.066
that you articulated

00:05:00.066 --> 00:05:01.734
or in a hypothetical where, okay,

00:05:01.734 --> 00:05:02.635
there's a group

00:05:02.635 --> 00:05:04.671
that seemingly is involved

00:05:04.671 --> 00:05:06.572
in some social justice profile

00:05:06.572 --> 00:05:08.241
but there's reason to believe

00:05:08.241 --> 00:05:09.842
that there will be trouble afoot

00:05:09.842 --> 00:05:12.779
from some of its members. Then what?

00:05:12.779 --> 00:05:15.782
Then I think you do have the ability

00:05:15.948 --> 00:05:17.784
to monitor it in that context,

00:05:17.784 --> 00:05:19.485
but you have to have rules around it.

00:05:19.485 --> 00:05:20.887
And I think that's where

00:05:20.887 --> 00:05:22.922
most police departments fall down,

00:05:23.156 --> 00:05:24.223
which is that the rules

00:05:24.223 --> 00:05:26.159
that they have are very permissive.

00:05:26.159 --> 00:05:27.060
They are just like,

00:05:27.060 --> 00:05:29.362
oh, social media monitoring is useful.

00:05:29.562 --> 00:05:30.963
You're allowed to do it.

00:05:30.963 --> 00:05:31.597
But in fact,

00:05:31.597 --> 00:05:33.366
if you're monitoring for public safety

00:05:33.366 --> 00:05:34.200
purposes, say,

00:05:34.200 --> 00:05:35.501
you know, to

00:05:35.501 --> 00:05:37.437
figure out what resources you need

00:05:37.437 --> 00:05:39.572
at a protest or where you might be afraid

00:05:39.572 --> 00:05:41.341
that there might be some violence,

00:05:41.341 --> 00:05:43.376
then once that protest is done,

00:05:43.376 --> 00:05:45.445
you don't need to keep that data anymore.

00:05:45.478 --> 00:05:47.547
You don't need to be creating dossiers

00:05:47.547 --> 00:05:49.849
on activists and protesters. Right.

00:05:50.116 --> 00:05:51.851
So it's really a question of how

00:05:51.851 --> 00:05:52.885
to make sure

00:05:52.885 --> 00:05:53.920
that police can use

00:05:53.920 --> 00:05:54.787
social media

00:05:54.787 --> 00:05:56.923
as an effective crime-fighting jewel,

00:05:57.156 --> 00:05:59.492
but not use it to infringe on people's

00:05:59.492 --> 00:06:01.427
First Amendment rights.

00:06:01.427 --> 00:06:03.896
You're reminding me of a reason I'm doing

00:06:03.896 --> 00:06:04.964
I'm on the fly here,

00:06:04.964 --> 00:06:05.898
but I think I'm

00:06:05.898 --> 00:06:07.567
right over the last couple of years,

00:06:07.567 --> 00:06:08.201
there was a

00:06:08.201 --> 00:06:09.602
there was a Supreme Court case.

00:06:09.602 --> 00:06:11.070
Tell me if you think that this is

00:06:11.070 --> 00:06:12.438
fair for comparison.

00:06:12.438 --> 00:06:14.640
And it said that if Pfizer Patel

00:06:14.640 --> 00:06:15.875
is driving down the street

00:06:15.875 --> 00:06:18.044
law enforcement can follow you

00:06:18.044 --> 00:06:20.113
as you go about your business in public.

00:06:20.279 --> 00:06:21.214
But in order to place

00:06:21.214 --> 00:06:23.516
a GPS tracker on your car,

00:06:23.716 --> 00:06:25.118
there's going to have to be a showing

00:06:25.118 --> 00:06:26.085
and a warrant

00:06:26.085 --> 00:06:28.521
So how does that compare to this?

00:06:28.521 --> 00:06:30.556
Because like this individual

00:06:30.690 --> 00:06:31.657
who's been charged

00:06:31.657 --> 00:06:32.458
in Philadelphia

00:06:32.458 --> 00:06:35.661
with 185,000 Instagram followers

00:06:35.895 --> 00:06:36.162
you know,

00:06:36.162 --> 00:06:38.598
is very publicly allegedly

00:06:38.898 --> 00:06:40.032
making the case.

00:06:40.032 --> 00:06:41.200
Here's where we should go.

00:06:41.200 --> 00:06:42.802
Here's what we want to hear next.

00:06:42.802 --> 00:06:45.271
And so in my analysis,

00:06:45.271 --> 00:06:47.006
that's the case of law enforcement

00:06:47.006 --> 00:06:48.574
following you down the street.

00:06:48.574 --> 00:06:50.243
They shouldn't need to do anything.

00:06:50.243 --> 00:06:51.744
They should be able to follow her

00:06:51.744 --> 00:06:52.612
like any of the other

00:06:52.612 --> 00:06:54.113
nearly 200,000 people

00:06:55.681 --> 00:06:56.916
So I think one of the

00:06:56.916 --> 00:06:58.050
things to understand

00:06:58.050 --> 00:06:59.986
about surveillance technology

00:06:59.986 --> 00:07:01.954
over the last couple of decades

00:07:01.954 --> 00:07:04.190
is that it has changed dramatically.

00:07:04.190 --> 00:07:04.424
Right.

00:07:04.424 --> 00:07:06.192
When we used to think about surveillance,

00:07:06.192 --> 00:07:07.927
we would think about wiretaps. Right.

00:07:07.927 --> 00:07:09.395
I call you.

00:07:09.395 --> 00:07:11.063
And if the police want to listen in,

00:07:11.063 --> 00:07:12.598
they need to get a warrant.

00:07:12.598 --> 00:07:14.000
Now, what we're looking at more

00:07:14.000 --> 00:07:16.068
and more is public surveillance. Right.

00:07:16.235 --> 00:07:18.271
Which is the ability of technology

00:07:18.271 --> 00:07:20.039
to track people

00:07:20.039 --> 00:07:22.975
generally without their knowing through,

00:07:22.975 --> 00:07:23.309
you know,

00:07:23.309 --> 00:07:24.877
cell phone, GPS

00:07:24.877 --> 00:07:27.046
those kinds of technologies.

00:07:27.046 --> 00:07:28.080
Social media falls

00:07:28.080 --> 00:07:29.582
in a slightly different place

00:07:29.582 --> 00:07:30.917
because in those instances,

00:07:30.917 --> 00:07:32.385
you're not actively trying

00:07:32.385 --> 00:07:34.620
to reveal your location to anybody

00:07:34.620 --> 00:07:35.788
you're just walking down the street.

00:07:35.788 --> 00:07:36.389
Right.

00:07:36.422 --> 00:07:37.323
Whereas social media,

00:07:37.323 --> 00:07:37.890
you're actually

00:07:37.890 --> 00:07:39.992
putting information out there.

00:07:39.992 --> 00:07:41.594
So I think it is reasonable

00:07:41.594 --> 00:07:43.996
to allow police access to social media.

00:07:43.996 --> 00:07:45.398
But at the same time,

00:07:45.398 --> 00:07:46.933
we need to make sure

00:07:46.933 --> 00:07:48.868
that we have protections in place

00:07:48.868 --> 00:07:51.404
so that that access is not used

00:07:51.604 --> 00:07:54.540
to monitor protesters and people who are

00:07:55.541 --> 00:07:56.843
you know, I get it.

00:07:56.843 --> 00:07:57.410
I get it.

00:07:57.410 --> 00:07:59.045
Let me let me just say

00:07:59.045 --> 00:08:00.079
by way of example

00:08:00.079 --> 00:08:01.214
and put this up on the screen,

00:08:01.214 --> 00:08:02.315
it's from The Philadelphia Inquirer

00:08:02.315 --> 00:08:03.115
this week about what

00:08:03.115 --> 00:08:04.350
just went on here blocks

00:08:04.350 --> 00:08:05.518
from where I'm seated.

00:08:05.518 --> 00:08:06.252
I mean, this is

00:08:06.252 --> 00:08:08.221
this was some of what was being said

00:08:08.221 --> 00:08:09.088
in social media,

00:08:09.088 --> 00:08:10.122
what time we go

00:08:10.122 --> 00:08:11.791
in, quote unquote, shopping,

00:08:11.791 --> 00:08:13.593
we looting or not

00:08:13.593 --> 00:08:15.461
I know they say tearing up our stuff

00:08:15.461 --> 00:08:16.295
ain't right.

00:08:16.295 --> 00:08:18.197
But that's the only way they hear us.

00:08:18.197 --> 00:08:19.065
I mean, I'm

00:08:19.065 --> 00:08:20.433
just hoping that there's a cop

00:08:20.433 --> 00:08:22.668
with a smartphone in a police van

00:08:22.935 --> 00:08:24.303
you know, on patrol

00:08:24.303 --> 00:08:25.705
who is also following this

00:08:25.705 --> 00:08:26.639
and saying, aha,

00:08:26.639 --> 00:08:28.007
here's where we need to go next.

00:08:28.007 --> 00:08:29.308
You get the final word.

00:08:29.308 --> 00:08:30.142
What did you most want to

00:08:30.142 --> 00:08:31.244
say in 30 seconds?

00:08:32.645 --> 00:08:33.246
I think that

00:08:33.246 --> 00:08:33.813
social media

00:08:33.813 --> 00:08:35.748
monitoring can be a useful tool

00:08:35.748 --> 00:08:37.083
for fighting crime,

00:08:37.083 --> 00:08:38.284
but I think it's really,

00:08:38.284 --> 00:08:39.051
really important

00:08:39.051 --> 00:08:40.052
that police departments

00:08:40.052 --> 00:08:42.488
have clear standards and safeguards

00:08:42.622 --> 00:08:45.791
so that that tool is not turned

00:08:45.791 --> 00:08:46.893
against Americans

00:08:46.893 --> 00:08:49.862
exercising their First Amendment rights.

00:08:49.862 --> 00:08:50.563
Pfizer Patel,

00:08:50.563 --> 00:08:51.764
thank you so much for being here.

